<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Revival Lê dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1533">1533</a>–<a href="/years/1789">1789</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Later Lê restoration</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Revival Lê dynasty was the restored Lê imperial line of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> from 1533 to 1789. After the Mạc seized the throne in 1527, loyalists under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nguyen-kim">Nguyễn Kim</a> set up a Lê emperor in Thanh Hóa, and this southern court fought the Mạc until it retook <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thang-long">Thăng Long</a> in 1592. Real power lay with the Trịnh lords in the north and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nguyen-lords">Nguyễn lords</a> in the south, and the line ended in 1789 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tay-son-dynasty">Tây Sơn</a> wars.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/revival-le-dynasty.jpg" alt="Revival Lê dynasty" width="320" height="533" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Created by CGlassey, based on province map of Vietnam. … · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:VietnamMac1540.gif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Primal Lê dynasty, founded in 1428, declined after the death of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/le-thanh-tong">Lê Thánh Tông</a>, and in 1527 the mandarin and general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mac-dang-dung">Mạc Đăng Dung</a> deposed Emperor Lê Cung Hoàng and founded the Mạc dynasty. Lê royalists escaped to the Kingdom of Lan Xang, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nguyen-kim">Nguyễn Kim</a> gathered those still loyal into a new army. In 1533 he made Prince Lê Duy Ninh emperor as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/le-trang-tong">Lê Trang Tông</a>, whom a Ming delegation recognised in 1536, and the Lê and Mạc fought on in the era of the Southern and Northern dynasties.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nguyen-kim">Nguyễn Kim</a> held real power until he was poisoned in 1545 by Dương Chấp Nhất, a surrendered Mạc general. Power at court passed to his son-in-law <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinh-kiem">Trịnh Kiểm</a>, founder of the Trịnh lords, while Nguyễn Kim's son Nguyễn Hoàng moved south as Viceroy of Thuận Hoá and became the first of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nguyen-lords">Nguyễn lords</a>. After 1592 the Mạc held out in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-bang">Cao Bằng</a> with the Ming as allies, and Lê Thế Tông gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinh-tung">Trịnh Tùng</a> the title Pacifying Prince. Emperors who crossed the Trịnh were removed: Lê Anh Tông was assassinated and Lê Kính Tông executed.</p><p>In the great war between the Trịnh and the Nguyễn, the Nguyễn completed two walls six metres high and eighteen kilometres long on their northern frontier in 1631 and, with Portuguese weapons, held off far larger Trịnh armies. After a failed offensive under Lê Gia Tông a peace followed that lasted about a hundred years, while the Nguyễn expanded south into lands of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cham">Cham</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Decades of war left the peasantry burdened by taxes that drove many off the land, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scholar-officials">scholar-officials</a>, exempt from land tax, acquired large estates. New taxes fell on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charcoal">charcoal</a>, salt, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cinnamon">cinnamon</a> and on fishing and mining, irrigation fell into disrepair, and flooding and famine followed. Numerous peasant revolts broke out between 1730 and 1770; most were local and supported the restoration of the Lê, while demanding land reform, more equitable taxes and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> for all.</p><p>The Portuguese, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>, English and French all had trading posts at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pho-hien">Phố Hiến</a> by 1680, all closed by 1700. The French Jesuit Alexandre de Rhodes, sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanoi">Hanoi</a> in 1627, reportedly baptised more than 6,000 converts before his expulsion in 1630 and is credited with perfecting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnamese-alphabet">Chữ Quốc ngữ</a>, the romanised script of Vietnamese. Woodcarving flourished, and many pieces are now in the National Museum in Hanoi.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Revival Lê dynasty” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Their opponents, led by Nguyen Kim and then Trinh Kiem, restored a Le emperor in 1533 in Thanh Hoa, beginning the war between the Northern Mac and <b>Southern Le</b> courts.</blockquote><a href="/state/mac-dynasty">Mac dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
